# sem-audiences — UX design sheet prompt
# Display modes: declaration chip strip; gated content in the three
# profile states (none / operator / reader); class-form alias; authoring
# error states (mixed spec, unknown token); JS-off all-visible.
# Reference attachments are the judged renders from the snap harness and
# the private enhanced fixture (see reports/tag-sem-audiences.md).

schema: "0.4"
id: sem-audiences-ux-sheet
type: image
quality: high

prompt:
  text: |
    A precise UX design sheet for the SemText vocabulary element
    `sem-audiences` and the `audience` qualifier (reader-profile-gated
    visibility), rendered as FIVE example pages in a grid on a dark
    minimal-tech canvas (background #0f172a, card surface #1e293b,
    hairline borders #334155, violet accent, blue note markers).

    PAGE 1 — THE DECLARATION STRIP: one quiet horizontal metadata row:
    a small-caps muted label "AUDIENCES ·" followed by one pill chip per
    profile ("Reader", "Operator") — 1px hairline border, fully rounded,
    surface background, small legible label text. This strip is metadata
    like a byline, not a control: no button affordance, no hover state,
    no checkmarks. Below it, two callout notes (left accent border,
    rounded card, prose fully readable) and one plain paragraph — all
    three visible together is NOT this page's point; page 1 shows the
    strip alone with a single generic line of prose beneath.

    PAGE 2 — NO PROFILE ACTIVE (fallback running): the same declaration
    strip on top; beneath it ONLY the negated note ("Everyone except
    operators…") is present. The operator-only note and the
    profile-listed paragraph are ABSENT from the page — the layout is
    tight, they left no gap, no box outline, no black bar, no grey blur.
    Hiding here means removal from flow, never occlusion.

    PAGE 3 — OPERATOR ACTIVE (reader picked `#sem-audience=operator`):
    the same strip; now the operator note and the shared paragraph are
    present, and the negated note is absent instead. Show a small muted
    URL caption above the page ("…#sem-audience=operator") — the switch
    is navigation, the page carries no picker UI.

    PAGE 4 — CLASS FORM, IDENTICAL RENDER: the same strip spelled as
    `div.sem-audiences > div.sem-profile` — visually indistinguishable
    from page 1: same label, same chips, same quiet metadata row.

    PAGE 5 — FAIL-OPEN ERROR STATE: a lone callout note qualified with a
    profile no block declares ("No reviewer profile is declared: warned,
    and shown regardless") renders fully visible beside a subtle monospace
    console-warn caption. Nothing is hidden for a typo — content must
    never be unreachable.

    Typography: clean humanist sans for prose, small-caps muted strip
    label, monospace only for the URL caption and console line. Flat,
    engineered, restrained — a documentation specimen, not an
    illustration.
  negative: |
    no occluded, blurred, black-barred, or question-mark-boxed text —
    hidden content is fully absent from flow, never redacted in place;
    no spoiler covers, no "click to reveal" buttons, no toggle switches
    or profile-picker dropdowns drawn as interactive controls, no
    highlighted or pressed state on the chips, no light-mode canvas,
    no decorative photos or icons, no gradient glows, no watermarks,
    no browser chrome, no empty reserved gaps where hidden content was.
  style: flat dark technical documentation specimen, minimal-tech design system

attachments:
  - path: /Users/keithbrings/Work/Space/Noizu/.tmp/semtext-docs-overhaul/snaps/sem-audiences/sem-audiences-primary-render-1280.png
    role: reference
  - path: /Users/keithbrings/Work/Space/Noizu/.tmp/semtext-docs-overhaul/snaps/sem-audiences/sem-audiences-qualifier-render-1280.png
    role: reference
  - path: /tmp/semtext-snap/sem-audiences/shots/aud-enhanced-no-profile.png
    role: reference
  - path: /tmp/semtext-snap/sem-audiences/shots/aud-enhanced-operator.png
    role: reference
  - path: /tmp/semtext-snap/sem-audiences/shots/aud-js-off.png
    role: reference

output:
  formats:
    - format: png
      filename: sem-audiences-ux-sheet.png
  dimensions:
    width: 2048
    height: 1152
    aspect_ratio: "16:9"

eval:
  pass_threshold: 0.7
  criteria:
    strip_metadata_not_control:
      weight: 0.25
      description: >
        The audiences strip reads as quiet metadata (label + hairline
        pill chips); nothing on it looks pressable, selected, or
        interactive.
      fail_signals:
        - chips rendered as buttons with shadows, hover, or pressed state
        - a dropdown, toggle, or picker drawn near the strip
    absence_not_occlusion:
      weight: 0.3
      description: >
        Gated-out content is completely absent — no boxes, gaps, bars,
        blurs, or covers where hidden notes would sit; visible text is
        fully legible.
      fail_signals:
        - blacked-out or blurred text blocks
        - reserved empty gaps inside a card layout
        - a spoiler or reveal control anywhere on a page
    profile_states_differ:
      weight: 0.2
      description: >
        The no-profile page and the operator page show complementary
        subsets of the same notes, and the operator page carries the
        hash-parameter caption.
      fail_signals:
        - both states showing all three qualified elements
        - a note visible in neither state
    form_parity:
      weight: 0.15
      description: >
        The class-form page is visually indistinguishable from the tag
        form strip.
      fail_signals:
        - chips styled differently on the class-form page
    fail_open_visible:
      weight: 0.1
      description: >
        The unknown-token note is fully rendered, not hidden, beside a
        console-warn caption.
      fail_signals:
        - the fail-open note missing or dimmed
  reject_if:
    - any text is occluded, blurred, or covered by an opaque block
    - light-mode canvas
    - watermark or browser chrome present

tags: [semtext, sem-audiences, ux-sheet, audience-gating, reference]
product_targets: [semtext.dev/reference]
